00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:47.010
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Specialized identifiers, or DIDs, are special identifiers that resolve to DID documents. Each DID document contains information about the subject of the DID, including authentication keys.

37
00:03:47.010 --> 00:04:01.760
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Authentication keys let you prove who you are when accessing a service. Imagine Alice logging into an email or work server, but instead of typing a password, she demonstrates control of a private key that pairs with the public key listed in her DID document.

38
00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:26.760
Audio shared by Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): The service checks her public key, and she proves she holds the matching private key, and that authenticates her identity with the service. Authentication keys are a cornerstone of how DIDS enable secure, verifiable identity. They allow a DIDS subject to interact with services directly while maintaining cryptographic proof of control. This is what makes DIDS so powerful. They combine decentralization, security, and flexibility.

