00:13:12.790 --> 00:13:27.039
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): I thought the key pre-rotation was perhaps a good script to stick your fact in about. So, keys and digital identity come in pairs, a private key and a public key. You keep the private key secret, usually stored in a secure digital wallet.

145
00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:33.879
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): The public key, on the other hand, is meant to be shared, for example, by publishing it in a DID document so that others can use it to verify you.

146
00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:52.850
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): With these keys, you can do powerful things. You can authenticate yourself online and log into services without passwords. You can also digitally sign documents, such as universities… such as a university signing degrees for its graduates, and anyone can check that signature against the public key. But there's a catch.

147
00:13:52.850 --> 00:14:04.030
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): If your private key is ever compromised, maybe your wallet gets hacked, or the storage system is tricked, your identity could be at risk, meaning that someone could impersonate you online.

148
00:14:04.030 --> 00:14:15.530
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): That's why it's good practice to rotate your keys, retire the old one, and start using a new one. In DID systems, these updates are recorded in the DID document, may be recorded in the DID document.

149
00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:36.030
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): No, they are. The latest update is recorded in the DID document, so that anyone resolving the DID knows which key is current. Still, that raises the problem. What if an attacker steals your key and performs a rotation before you can? Suddenly, they look like the rightful controller of the DID, and you can't reclaim control because the system now trusts their key.

150
00:14:36.190 --> 00:14:51.310
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): This is where key pre-rotation comes in. With pre-rotation, every time you create a key, you also generate the next one in advance. Instead of publishing the full future key, you publish a cryptographic digest, a commitment to the key you'll use next.

151
00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:58.970
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Later, when you rotate, you reveal the actual key, and anyone can check that it matches the digest you committed to earlier.

152
00:14:59.020 --> 00:15:17.639
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Where this pre-rotation data is stored is specific to each DID method, and is not part of the DID core specification. With some methods, like DIDWebS and DIDWebVH, support… some… some DID methods, like DIDWebVS and DIDWebVH, support key pre-rotation, and others do not.

153
00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:34.619
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): With key pre-rotation, even if someone compromises your current private key, they can't hijack your DID because they don't know the next key you've already committed to. We are supported. Pre-rotation makes key rotation predictable and tamper-resistant, securing DIDs against takeover.

